#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116453 :tutorial/content "Consider the right triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f7de9810f4acf33b504eb010d641e99811de82eed919e4340b5e68d414269ca.png)\n\nThe area of this right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac 1 2 ab.\n$$\n\nBut how can we calculate the area if we only know one side and one angle, as in the triangle below? \n\n![](images/c1/c1e5331333639f4d8e4f7b4cb3fee30ed1a7a889968844ae2b10961c5ae8e3ef.png)\n\nWe can follow the two steps below.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the missing side using the trigonometric ratios.\n\nHere, we need the adjacent side, and we know the opposite side. So, we should use tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{3}{BC} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & =\\frac{3}{BC} \\\\ BC\\sqrt{3} & =9 \\\\ BC & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ BC & =3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Use the formula to calculate the area.\n\nNow that we know both sides of the triangle, we can just apply the formula as usual and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "96ace952-d580-563b-85f2-45a48227303d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2458 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
